enemies  for the first time her help stops when  you start cutting your teeth on high  rank monsters but hearing about new  skills and immediately putting them into  practice in the field is an excellent  way to learn about the game from the  ground up now once our world was always  promised as an open wolf experience to  some extent it delivers on that  fantastically large amounts of a scale  that we haven't seen before both  vertically and horizontally no  discernable game pausing loading screens  between zones and hunting areas and a  wealth of beautifully rendered  environments to slaughter colossal  monsters in  [Music]  out of the ability to slaughter said  monsters is the smart use of all the  tools in your hunting arsenal with the  most important being your weapon of  choice the hunter arts from Monster  Hunter generations have been removed and  the game's focus is solely on your  ability to dish out ridiculous amounts  of damage  using your respective weapons combo  light weapons are still the most mobile  while the technical weapons are still  the most difficult to understand and  master but there are ample opportunities  to get experience with whatever blade  bow or Lance you've decided on you'll  also make use of environmental hazards  traps barrel bombs and dung in order to  chase down your quarry there are many  different ways to get a leg up on  monsters which make encounters more  accessible to different playstyles  aerial mounting and elemental effects  are still all the rage with weapons  boasting essential new perks that  evolved alongside the enemies that  you've forged them from but the game  will reward you no matter what  strategies you take  multi-plane integration is for the most  part seamless as mentioned before  there's no distinction between village  and guild quests anymore submissions can  be done alone or with a friend and  you'll both only have to do it once to  complete it you can start a quest alone  in an online session and wait for some  mates to pop in to assist alternatively  you can seek out an online session for  people of a certain hunter rank and just  go along for the ride if they need a  hand with anything the difficulty will  scale depending on the amount of people  and the only qualifier is that some  story focused missions require the  leader to either watch a cutscene or  discover a monster before others can  join getting together with your mates  does take a few extra steps compared to  loading into a session with a stranger  if you know you've got a friend online  you'll have to join in on their fun via  the friends list on your consoles  dashboard while you can drop in and out  of public sessions with strangers easily  getting people to specifically join your  online session requires them to enter a  12-digit session ID in a game that's all  about momentum and sprinting off into  the horizon at the next challenge  getting your Posse together is  manageable but slightly tedious that  being said spending a few minutes to set  up a multiplayer session doesn't  necessarily make or break the game  there's also the ability to be in the  same online session as someone else  without actually having to do the quests  that they're doing which is useful for  those who might want to keep an eye on a  friend who's new to the franchise  players who are also struggling solo can  send out an SOS flare that lets others  put together a little rescue party to  save the day  now while it may seem that quite a bit  has changed there's also a hell of a lot  in monster underworld that has stayed  the same whether it's the appearance of  familiar monsters or the tried and true  weapons the roots of the Monster Hunter  franchise are strong with its latest  release apart from the overall sprucing  up of the graphics and the cutscenes  with full voiceovers the standout  improvements really come from the  simplification of existing systems in a  way that welcomes newcomers without  alienating existing fans a lack of  loading screens makes exploration of  pleasure and tracking new and improved  monsters through errors as they rank up  means that you've got plenty to conquer  once the story quests are complete there  may not be any new weapons and there may  be a hunter art sized hole left in the  hearts of players who spent hours  getting good at the various styles  however the removal of these old  mechanics feels less like a funeral and  more like a necessary streamlining  [Music]  ever since the title was first announced  last year it was clear that Capcom was  gunning for something grander than  Monster Hunter generations it has  succeeded and this is likely the biggest  and best that the franchise has ever  been it's not just the competitive depth  of the narrative it also boasts almost  seamless integration between combat  systems that were previously  incomprehensible for amateurs the  Monster Hunter formula has definitely  heard its claws and all the  aforementioned factors play their part  in making monster onto world a  meaningful evolution for the series at  large  [Music]  [Music]
 

